1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
So please join me in welcoming Negar Mehr.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:11,440
Thank you so much, Ken, for the very kind introduction and thanks to the organizers for bringing us together.

3
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:16,690
So I'm very excited to share with you what my lab has been working on.

4
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:21,450
And in particular, if you're worried that there is not much left for you to do in robotics,

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:27,370
I want to encourage you all to think about the next steps and I want to convince you that there are still

6
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:36,180
many more problems and challenges to solve So we are all excited We are seeing performances capabilities and robotics that we couldn't

7
00:00:36,230 --> 00:00:43,700
really imagine of a couple of years ago Every time I just open up my Twitter or X I'm impressed by the

8
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:51,560
demos that we see and the exciting capabilities that are being unlocked by the robotic systems But I think as a community,

9
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:59,730
it is now time to think about how can we go one step further and how can we have robots that are integrated into our lives,

10
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:08,440
the same way that autonomous cars were integrated into our lives and they share roads with human driven cars or pedestrians around here in Austin,

11
00:01:08,490 --> 00:01:11,370
I see many Waymo cars, which is very exciting.

12
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:16,340
But now that we have all these new capabilities emerging in robotic systems,

13
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:22,100
how about having humanoids, quadrupeds or other robots around in this conference helping us.

14
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:26,110
And obviously we are very far from that point and that is why I had

15
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,860
to generate this image using Astra because I couldn't find any real world images of this.

16
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:36,530
But what we are looking at is how can we get robots to interact safely

17
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:42,870
and intelligently with other agents in unstructured environments that are humans around or other robots around.

18
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:49,630
And that's, I think, a very timely question to ask, because maybe five years ago when I posed this question,

19
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:54,710
people would be like, but I don't even have a robot that does things reliably on its own.

20
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:56,340
Why would I worry about this?

21
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,790
But I think it's time to think about this.

22
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:07,350
And this is a very hard problem that requires us to think about different aspects of robotic algorithm design and hardware design.

23
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:13,960
On the algorithmic side, my lab has been working on what does planning look like in these domains?

24
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:15,620
What should learning look like?

25
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:16,760
How about perception?

26
00:02:16,810 --> 00:02:17,830
How about safety?

27
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:25,630
But we are at conference on robot learning, so I want to share with you in what ways is learning in these domains different,

28
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:33,230
and in what ways some lessons that we have learned from learning in multi-agent domains can actually be useful for learning in single-agent domains.

29
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:40,810
And throughout this talk, to motivate what we do, I'm going to use a very simple running example.

30
00:02:41,650 --> 00:02:49,070
Imagine you have two agents, two robots, both of whom want to carry an object like a basket.

31
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:57,770
And what I'm interested in is that I want to learn policies for each one of those robots so that when I pair these policies together,

32
00:02:57,990 --> 00:03:03,110
they collectively succeed in this task of collaborating an object on transporting an object.

33
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,980
What's hard about this is that I want my policies to be decentralized.

34
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:10,910
I want every robot to make decisions based on its own local observations,

35
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:17,080
but there is some coupling which is implicit that both of them should agree on how to do a task to succeed.

36
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:19,510
What are our approaches?

37
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:22,240
What are the recipes out there for learning these policies?

38
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:24,720
One approach is through imitation learning.

39
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:27,160
One is through reinforcement learning.

40
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:33,560
And what I'm going to do today is first I'm going to talk about what imitation looks like in these domains.

41
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:38,580
Then I'm going to share with you what happens if you try to use RL for solving these tasks.

42
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:42,390
And finally, I'm going to share some recent advances that we've made,

43
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,990
which I think are very exciting and tell us how ideas from multi

44
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:50,900
agent learning can actually really help in the context of single-agent continual learning.

45
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,260
Let's start by imitating.

46
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:58,600
We've seen a lot of capabilities?

47
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:04,680
What if I just collect some demonstrations from two robots collaboratively lifting an object?

48
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:09,310
And then I can just do behavioral cloning and directly imitate policies.

49
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,840
How is it even different from single-agent domains?

50
00:04:12,530 --> 00:04:17,300
So we started to think about this problem theoretically first to think about in

51
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:23,030
what ways is directly imitating policies in multi-agent domains different from single-agent domains.

52
00:04:23,870 --> 00:04:27,670
And intuitively, at a very high level, the difficulties are following.

53
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:33,260
Let's say I collect some demonstration data, and my object is a heavy object.

54
00:04:33,310 --> 00:04:36,910
So no single robot can on its own pick up the object.

55
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:40,240
And then I imitate policies of the two robots.

56
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:49,000
But during execution, if one of the robots slightly deviates from what it did, or what the expert did in its demonstrations,

57
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:55,950
the entire effort fails, and the other robot, no matter how hard it tries, it can't really account for what happened.

58
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,090
So the deviations from expert expert policies, which are very common in behavioral cloning,

59
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:05,540
are something that we cannot really tolerate and accommodate in multi-agent domains.

60
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:11,050
So again, we formally characterize this, it turns out that instead of value gap,

61
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:17,510
which is the metric that we typically care about in single-agent imitation learning, in multi-agent behavioral cloning,

62
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:22,340
we have to think about something that we call exploitability of learned policies, which is,

63
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:30,360
if one agent deviates slightly from what they did in the expert demo, how will the other agent react and how bad that can be.

64
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:35,950
And again, very informally, it's very hard to learn low exploitable policies.

65
00:05:36,110 --> 00:05:41,760
And we have noticed that what this theoretical results means is that data coverage,

66
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:49,780
covering all possible failure modes and all possible interaction modes matters even more in multi-agent interaction.

67
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:52,890
So you have to scale up your data collection even more.

68
00:05:53,390 --> 00:06:00,710
And yet, all this is based on the assumption that there is only one way to coordinate with other agents.

69
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:05,060
And we know that interactions are multimodal.

70
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:10,470
multimodality in multi-agent interactions is even more common than single-agent domains.

71
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:17,210
The same task that I showed, there are two ways for the robots or even more, you could argue there are three ways for the robots to coordinate.

72
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:23,740
But how can you how can they decide on their own which mode the other agent is picking up on?

73
00:06:24,590 --> 00:06:30,490
So the moment I talk about multimodalities, I'm sure this audience thinks of diffusion policies,

74
00:06:30,650 --> 00:06:36,650
because because we've all seen how amazing diffusion policies have been in capturing multimodalities.

75
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:42,780
So we started to think, maybe that's the recipe, maybe to learn a different coordination mode,

76
00:06:42,830 --> 00:06:47,540
what I need to do is to use the multimodal capabilities of diffusion

77
00:06:47,590 --> 00:06:53,150
policies and imitate decentralized diffusion policies that can learn to adapt to other agents.

78
00:06:53,830 --> 00:07:01,580
So we designed a centralized training, decentralized execution pipeline where we pair policies with one another,

79
00:07:01,630 --> 00:07:06,970
train them, but during execution they're just decentralized policies all in a diffusion form.

80
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:08,560
And it works.

81
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:15,040
One of my favorite videos is actually this video that the two robots are making decisions, each one of them is making decisions on its own,

82
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:20,950
but they don't know which way the other robot is going, but they learn to implicitly coordinate with one another.

83
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,970
One of them is pulling the basket, the other is pushing, but by just leveraging this to cast this at the end,

84
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:30,640
running this in a receding horizon, they ultimately agree on a one mode of interaction.

85
00:07:31,610 --> 00:07:32,490
So this is great.

86
00:07:33,010 --> 00:07:34,010
Is it solved?

87
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:39,710
For any of you who has ever teleoperated the bi-manual setup or a multi-agent setup,

88
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:45,000
you kind of know how hard it is to collect data in these interactive domains.

89
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:51,940
So this requires enough demonstration data for each interactive task, which is really hard to achieve.

90
00:07:52,790 --> 00:07:55,090
And naturally for all of us in this conference,

91
00:07:55,450 --> 00:08:03,680
a very key question to ask is single-agent robots have so much capability that are pre-trained in them these days.

92
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:12,170
How can we build on these pre-trained skills that robots have achieved, to enable robots to have coordination on demand?

93
00:08:12,790 --> 00:08:17,710
Instead of training everything from scratch for my robots to learn how to coordinate with one another?

94
00:08:17,950 --> 00:08:24,780
What if I just build on the existing capabilities and make the robot coordinate and interact only when it is needed,

95
00:08:24,830 --> 00:08:28,630
and not forgetting about things that they could do on on their own?

96
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:34,900
So recently, we've been looking into what if we freeze what the robot already knows,

97
00:08:34,950 --> 00:08:41,230
and just learn only the correction that is needed for achieving coordination between pre-trained skills,

98
00:08:41,470 --> 00:08:46,610
only using few demonstrations, not as much as training everything from scratch.

99
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:52,530
And the first thing that they've tried is how about we just learn residual denoiser

100
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:58,740
for correcting single-agent policies to get robots to interact and coordinate with one another.

101
00:08:59,870 --> 00:09:06,950
So I assume that I have a diffusion policy that has learned certain skills for just one robot, let's say pick in place and wiping.

102
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:10,670
And what I do is just only few demonstration data,

103
00:09:10,830 --> 00:09:18,330
I use that to correct my denoising process so that I coordinate the timing of the agents actions with one another.

104
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:24,190
And the way that I do this is that I start from a frozen base policy for just one robot,

105
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:28,780
and collect very few demonstrations of multi-agent interaction,

106
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:39,020
then collect some distilled single-agent demonstrations from my frozen policy, and collectively use these data sets to train a coordination head.

107
00:09:39,730 --> 00:09:47,330
And what's nice about this coordination residual that I train is that I can achieve coordination when it is needed.

108
00:09:47,630 --> 00:09:56,680
One robot can do part of the job while the other one finishes the job, but also it retains the single-agent source behavior that the robots had.

109
00:09:56,730 --> 00:10:03,390
if a task just requires one robot to accomplish a task on its own, I haven't lost those capabilities.

110
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:10,420
But I want to encourage you to think about what we can do by building on these single-agent capabilities,

111
00:10:10,470 --> 00:10:14,600
because I think it's an exciting time to think about multi-agent coordination and

112
00:10:14,650 --> 00:10:20,430
integrating robots in these type of tasks using the capabilities that they have already built.

113
00:10:22,270 --> 00:10:24,560
So, imitation one way.

114
00:10:24,610 --> 00:10:29,210
What if I was trying to solve this same task using reinforcement learning.

115
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:38,100
We asked that question, if I use multi-agent reinforcement learning to train my two robots to collaboratively pick up this object,

116
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:44,960
this is the best that we could achieve, no matter how hard of a reward engineering with it.

117
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:52,700
No matter how hard we tried it, this was the best that the robots could do in terms of collaboratively picking up this object.

118
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:55,830
So we started to think, why is that the case?

119
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:58,900
Why is it so hard to use RL to train these policies.

120
00:10:59,220 --> 00:11:05,350
Why is it that we as humans are really good at interacting with one another learning to cooperate and interact with our

121
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:09,650
teammates in actually very intricate and complicated forms of interactions like

122
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:15,980
sports and very intricate forms of interactions that people build over time.

123
00:11:17,170 --> 00:11:20,940
And for those of you who have watched World Cup over this past summer,

124
00:11:20,990 --> 00:11:27,920
you perhaps remember how much everyone was talking about coaches and how all these memes about coaches went viral,

125
00:11:27,970 --> 00:11:34,370
and people were arguing about what's the role of a coach and coaches' strategy in a team's performance.

126
00:11:34,810 --> 00:11:42,130
So we started to think maybe one of the reasons that we humans can learn to interact with others is that we leverage good coaches.

127
00:11:42,450 --> 00:11:48,770
What if I can coach my robot to learn complex tasks like coordination tasks?

128
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:56,780
What if I use my foundation models to act as my coaches for guiding my robot learning loop?

129
00:11:57,080 --> 00:12:01,530
Using reasoning, coding, and visual understanding capabilities of these models,

130
00:12:01,580 --> 00:12:08,380
I just get to have a coach which is for free and autonomously guiding the learning process of my robot.

131
00:12:09,190 --> 00:12:16,110
Let's first see what that looks like in a single-agent setting, and then I bring in the multi-agent coordination aspect of it.

132
00:12:16,270 --> 00:12:19,070
Let's say I want to teach a robot to run backwards.

133
00:12:19,710 --> 00:12:24,140
And I'm not a locomotion person, so I don't really know what the reward function should be.

134
00:12:24,190 --> 00:12:26,550
I have no idea how I should set up the training.

135
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:34,840
So what we did was was like, okay, I feel have a coach, maybe my coach can break down the task into a sequence of simpler tasks.

136
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:41,780
Then my coach also defines what it means means to do each sub task such as, for example, is stabilizing itself.

137
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:44,070
And then my coach provides feedback.

138
00:12:44,120 --> 00:12:50,930
and that loop is gonna be enough for me to learn a good enough policy that they can transfer zero-shot to

139
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:59,080
my to my hardware and that way I can learn these skills in reinforcement learning without having to spend so much time figuring out

140
00:12:59,130 --> 00:13:07,290
what reward function I should try, and once I learn certain skills, let's say running backwards or some whole-body control with proper

141
00:13:07,340 --> 00:13:17,980
coaching I can actually use the existing skills that the robot has learned to accomplish new tasks with no training and no data collection.

142
00:13:18,820 --> 00:13:25,760
And inspired by the line of work that actually Ken pioneered on using code as policies or agentic robotics,

143
00:13:26,120 --> 00:13:31,980
we started to think what if I use code as a coach for composing the pre-trained robot skills.

144
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:39,790
For example, I have a robot that has its own let's say pre-trained skills such as walking backwards or a whole-body controller.

145
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:45,780
And I just use my coach in terms of a policy code to compose those skills with one another.

146
00:13:46,020 --> 00:13:49,680
And what's nice about this is that it doesn't require any retraining.

147
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:52,090
I don't collect any human data.

148
00:13:52,140 --> 00:13:57,480
And all it takes is just building on the pre-trained and already learned skills.

149
00:13:57,760 --> 00:14:00,490
And that to our surprise turned out to work,

150
00:14:00,540 --> 00:14:08,050
we could transfer these policies to the real world humanoid could essentially lift the basket and object from different positions,

151
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:10,680
different initial positions and different goal positions.

152
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:14,990
And what was even more interesting to me is that in other tasks, for example,

153
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:20,810
let's say pushing a button, when we first deployed our policy, we had around 20 % success rate.

154
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:28,090
But by just two rounds of refinement on our coach, coach code, we increase the success rate to 90%.

155
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:33,710
And this is all without any retargeting or human data collection, or anything like that.

156
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:38,860
And this is this was all by the way before Astra, I'm sure the performance is going to even be better now.

157
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:40,840
But this is for single-agent.

158
00:14:40,890 --> 00:14:45,290
And I promise to you that this is interesting for coordination and multi-agent systems.

159
00:14:45,770 --> 00:14:52,190
Turns out the same recipe actually enables coordination tasks that we couldn't really achieve before.

160
00:14:52,530 --> 00:14:53,900
Like, for example,

161
00:14:53,950 --> 00:15:02,300
this running example that I had collaboratively lifting an object with a proper coaching using these foundation models is doing a much,

162
00:15:02,350 --> 00:15:03,090
much better job.

163
00:15:03,690 --> 00:15:11,240
Or I can get my quadrupeds to essentially balance a seesaw so that one of them can climb the seesaw.

164
00:15:11,290 --> 00:15:16,530
Whereas if you try to just train multi-agent PPO to get to do this, it's hopeless.

165
00:15:17,320 --> 00:15:24,990
Or I can teach quadrupeds to pass through a gate by proper coaching of knowing, well, you should do this,

166
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:28,830
here's how you should do, here's what your reward should be, and here's what each sub task should be.

167
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:37,670
Or I can pair these robots with humans to collaboratively do tasks, such as transporting an object, which was really hard for me to do on my own.

168
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:44,360
But when I actually tried this experiment, I was surprised by how easy it is to actually have a quadruped helping me with transporting heavy objects.

169
00:15:45,750 --> 00:15:49,330
So this was very, to our surprise, it was very effective.

170
00:15:49,830 --> 00:15:52,020
And we were kind of inspired and encouraged by that.

171
00:15:52,070 --> 00:15:55,840
So we started to think, is there anything else that a coach does for a team of agents?

172
00:15:55,890 --> 00:16:01,200
And for anyone who has ever taught a class with group projects,

173
00:16:01,250 --> 00:16:06,660
I'm sure at the end of a class you've got complaints about I contributed to my team effort,

174
00:16:06,710 --> 00:16:10,450
my partners didn't, I should get credit for what I did, others shouldn't.

175
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:14,460
And that's the same thing that makes multi-agent reinforcement learning really hard,

176
00:16:14,510 --> 00:16:26,210
which is, as you're looking at how interactions unfold, you need to have a good understanding of who contributed to get credit.

177
00:16:26,510 --> 00:16:34,020
Because even if a team effort failed, there might still be some agents who actually contributed to the team effort,

178
00:16:34,070 --> 00:16:37,970
and others are the ones who essentially resulted in the team failure.

179
00:16:38,470 --> 00:16:45,460
So we're like, maybe I can just make my foundation model and my coach to just be a good credit designer.

180
00:16:45,510 --> 00:16:49,950
Maybe that's all I need for getting the robots to learn to coordinate with one another.

181
00:16:50,390 --> 00:16:52,360
And again, this was in 2025.

182
00:16:52,410 --> 00:16:55,180
Models were not as amazing as they are today.

183
00:16:55,230 --> 00:17:02,170
But it turned out by just simply asking the model existing model off the shelf model to play the role of a credit designer,

184
00:17:02,510 --> 00:17:08,370
you can outperform a state of the art in terms of a coordination capabilities by orders of magnitude.

185
00:17:08,790 --> 00:17:14,790
For example, in a warehouse where robots are moving objects around, and you really want to coordinate that effort,

186
00:17:15,130 --> 00:17:24,410
we are doing way better than a baseline such as MAPPO, HAPPO and LIPO, which are very well known baselines in multi-agent reinforcement learning.

187
00:17:24,870 --> 00:17:26,000
And again, this was 2025.

188
00:17:26,050 --> 00:17:28,480
I'm sure the performance is going to be even better now.

189
00:17:28,530 --> 00:17:36,160
But I want to argue that these are actually exciting times for us to think about how we can coach robots to learn to interact with one another,

190
00:17:36,210 --> 00:17:40,850
and hopefully down the line, cooperate and coordinate with humans.

191
00:17:42,170 --> 00:17:45,470
But now we want to flip the problem a little bit.

192
00:17:45,930 --> 00:17:51,180
And I want to argue that there are some lessons that we have learned by studying multi-agent systems,

193
00:17:51,230 --> 00:17:56,750
which have actually proven to be really useful and relevant for single-agent learning.

194
00:17:57,800 --> 00:18:01,200
And that has been primarily in the context of continual learning.

195
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:10,900
So let's now go back to the world of one agent learning on its own and let's imagine an agent that is continuously learning new tasks.

196
00:18:11,420 --> 00:18:16,530
So you start with some task T, then a new task T plus one comes, then another task comes.

197
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:20,560
What happens as a robot is continuously learning?

198
00:18:21,540 --> 00:18:27,500
Typically, what happens is that a robot learns a new task, but it forgets about what it had learned in the past.

199
00:18:28,100 --> 00:18:37,890
So, for example, I have a policy theta one that has learned to do one task T1, but I fine-tuned my policy on some tasks T2,

200
00:18:37,940 --> 00:18:46,500
then what happens is perhaps parameters that were really important for accomplishing task might be overridden by fine-tuning to task T2.

201
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:52,080
Same thing for task T3, as I move on, I may forget about what I had learned before.

202
00:18:52,680 --> 00:19:01,810
So we were facing this problem of catastrophic forgetting when you continuously learn new tasks, and we were thinking this is not good.

203
00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:05,960
I learned my task T3, but I forget about how I did tasks T1 and T2.

204
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:15,010
So with that multi-agent view of the we started to think what if continuously learning new tasks can be

205
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:21,200
cast as some sort of a consensus between what the robot is learning now and what it had learned before.

206
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:28,610
In a way, what if the robot didn't forget where it came from and it kept remembering who it was,

207
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:31,620
where it came from, very much the same as what a human should do, I think.

208
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:32,770
So,

209
00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:39,970
we started thinking about maybe we can cast continual learning as a temporal consensus between what

210
00:19:40,020 --> 00:19:44,780
the robot has learned before and what the new policy parameters of the robot should be.

211
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:51,050
And this is nice because with this consensus view of continuous learning,

212
00:19:51,100 --> 00:19:58,510
we can enable a replay-free policy update where I don't require to just keep track of data that was used in my pre

213
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:05,500
-training or hope that an industry lab releases the data set to so that we can keep track of what the robot had already learned.

214
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:12,250
So I enforce a consensus in the weight space of my policy parameters.

215
00:20:12,300 --> 00:20:15,650
When I'm learning my policy for task T plus 2,

216
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:25,340
I'm like there should be some form of an agreement with policy parameters for task T plus 1 and policy parameters for task T or T plus 1.

217
00:20:26,150 --> 00:20:32,630
And at a very high level intuitively what this does is that as I'm fine-tuning my policy to task T two,

218
00:20:32,950 --> 00:20:41,970
I kind of memorize and remember what policy parameters were really important for task T1, so I don't change them too much.

219
00:20:42,170 --> 00:20:49,400
And as I add these new tasks, I preserve the policy parameters that were really important and relevant to prior tasks,

220
00:20:49,450 --> 00:20:54,830
so that ultimately I can remember the prior tasks and adapt and learn the new task.

221
00:20:55,770 --> 00:20:57,340
But what does this consensus look like?

222
00:20:57,390 --> 00:20:58,280
So,

223
00:20:58,330 --> 00:21:05,240
let's say I've learned my policy parameters for task T and then I have a notion of a weight or importance weight

224
00:21:05,290 --> 00:21:14,100
for how much each policy parameter matters for task T and I have another task T plus one and the same thing,

225
00:21:14,150 --> 00:21:19,690
I have another notion of a weight matrix which is how much each parameter matters for task T plus one.

226
00:21:20,270 --> 00:21:28,580
Ideally, I want my policy parameters to be proportional to kind of like a weighted average of these policy parameters.

227
00:21:28,630 --> 00:21:32,730
That's what I mean by consensus in the policy parameter space.

228
00:21:34,100 --> 00:21:41,500
But these weights depend on policy parameters, and every task may require its own importance, so how do we achieve this?

229
00:21:42,700 --> 00:21:43,430
We were actually, again,

230
00:21:43,480 --> 00:21:47,670
inspired by the algorithms that are out there in multi-agent world back in the time

231
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:54,590
from 2014 that people have shown that you could actually use methods like a method of multipliers,

232
00:21:54,640 --> 00:21:55,590
primal-dual updates.

233
00:21:55,640 --> 00:21:59,840
For those of you have background in optimization, there's room for you to contribute to robotics.

234
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:02,590
So we started to think method of multipliers,

235
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:08,640
primal dual updates for reasons that are very well known in the community could actually be super useful for this task.

236
00:22:09,460 --> 00:22:14,970
And this results in kind of like an iterative update where you update your policy parameters a little bit,

237
00:22:15,020 --> 00:22:17,830
then you kind of move your consensus constraint a little bit.

238
00:22:17,880 --> 00:22:24,190
But in a nutshell, that results in kind of having a moving anchor for your memory as you're training your policies.

239
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:31,880
I'm not going to get into the details of how primal dual updates for method of multipliers work, but let me show you how this works in practice.

240
00:22:32,660 --> 00:22:41,110
We took π0.5; our pre-training tasks for π0.5 were a bunch of tasks that we ran in the lab, we collected some data from it,

241
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:48,260
but then we had some target tasks such as remove the lid and place the bird in the basket or cake on the cake tray.

242
00:22:49,440 --> 00:22:58,550
And we kind of compared the performance of this temporal consensus way of fine-tuning as opposed to baselines and supervised fine-tuning,

243
00:22:58,600 --> 00:23:00,640
the vanilla form of supervised fine-tuning.

244
00:23:01,230 --> 00:23:04,530
On the target tasks, both methods do well.

245
00:23:04,950 --> 00:23:08,630
Both of them learn the target task for which they have been fine-tuned to.

246
00:23:08,850 --> 00:23:14,290
But the beauty of temporal consensus view of it becomes clear when you look at the performance on the pre-trained tasks.

247
00:23:15,510 --> 00:23:24,720
While SFT forgets about what it had learned before, by just enforcing a simple consensus which is replay free in the policy parameter space,

248
00:23:24,770 --> 00:23:27,730
we managed to remember what the robot had learned before.

249
00:23:28,860 --> 00:23:32,930
And this is not me just cherry-picking what I really wanted you to see.

250
00:23:32,980 --> 00:23:42,320
We have kind of verified this and run a set of comparisons with other baselines out there, including replay or regularization based methods like EWC,

251
00:23:42,620 --> 00:23:48,540
but across them we found that this is the best in terms of adaptation while retaining prior skills.

252
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:54,830
And it's not just in the context of fine tuning that we see this method to be actually particularly useful.

253
00:23:54,880 --> 00:23:57,920
We see that in the context of mapping an environment as well.

254
00:23:58,500 --> 00:24:02,460
Let's say a robot is mapping an environment which is dynamically changing.

255
00:24:03,100 --> 00:24:08,720
And let's say some objects like a table has moved around as the robot is moving around and trying to map the environment.

256
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:19,740
If we use a replay based method, our mapping ends up with two copies of the same table because it has seen data of both positions of table.

257
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:26,030
And if we use a regularization based method that the robot fails to see that the table has moved around

258
00:24:26,080 --> 00:24:33,080
because it's just so regularized to not depart from what it has learned before that it doesn't learn to adapt.

259
00:24:33,360 --> 00:24:36,040
But by just this consensus view of things,

260
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:42,300
we can preserve things that haven't changed from the past and adapt to the parameters and changes in the scene.

261
00:24:43,170 --> 00:24:52,230
The same thing we saw in a number of experiments that we ran in the lab with a temporal consensus that my student calls his method TACO,

262
00:24:52,390 --> 00:25:00,080
we found that the same thing happens with replay methods, replay based methods, we kind of have the same copy or two copies of the same object.

263
00:25:00,130 --> 00:25:06,270
Whereas with regularization based methods, we essentially fail to adapt to the new changes in the environment.

264
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:10,650
So these are just just being very recent results that we found out.

265
00:25:10,700 --> 00:25:17,710
But I want to communicate that what we found so far is that this consensus view of continual learning has really

266
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:24,920
been really strong in balancing new skills and learning new skills with retaining what the robot has learned in the past.

267
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:29,410
And just recently we have seen that in the context of reinforcement learning it turns out you can do some really

268
00:25:29,460 --> 00:25:36,860
smart things in a within the the RL loop to adapt to changes and it's become super useful in fine-tuning policies.

